{"step_index":91,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T10:19:28Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-15T13:19:28+03:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-15T13:19:32+03:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-15T13:19:28+03:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-15T13:19:32+03:00\nThe search for \"\"hyprpaper\" symlink wallpaper\" returned the following summary:\nUsing a **symlink (symbolic link)** with `hyprpaper` is a popular technique for managing wallpapers, especially if you want to:\n1. Easily change your wallpaper dynamically via scripts[1] without rewriting your main `hyprpaper.conf` every time.\n2. Maintain a persistent \"current wallpaper\" state across reboots[1].\n\nHere is how you can set up and use a symlink workflow with `hyprpaper`.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Create a Static Symlink Path\nInstead of pointing `hyprpaper` directly to a constantly changing filename or deep directory, point it to a generic symlink path (e.g., `~/.config/hypr/wallpaper.png` or a state directory)[1].\n\nOpen your terminal and link an initial wallpaper to a fixed target path:\n```bash\n# Syntax: ln -sf /path/to/your/actual_image.png /path/to/symlink.png\nln -sf ~/Pictures/wallpapers/mountain.png ~/.config/hypr/current_wallpaper.png\n```\n\n### Step 2: Configure `hyprpaper.conf`\nPoint your `hyprpaper` configuration file (`~/.config/hypr/hyprpaper.conf`) to use that exact symlink[2][3]:\n\n```ini\npreload = /home/yourusername/.config/hypr/current_wallpaper.png\n\n# Set it for your monitor (replace eDP-1 with your actual monitor name from `hyprctl monitors`)\nwallpaper = eDP-1, /home/yourusername/.config/hypr/current_wallpaper.png\n\nsplash = false\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Changing Wallpapers Dynamically (The Real Power of Symlinks)\n\nBecause `hyprpaper` reads the file pointed to by the symlink, you can write a simple shell script to switch wallpapers on the fly[1]. When you change the symlink and tell `hyprpaper` to reload, it will instantly display the new image.\n\nCreate a script (e.g., `~/bin/switch-wallpaper.sh`):\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# 1. Pick a new wallpaper (using an image picker like rofi, or finding a random file in a folder)\nWALLPAPER_DIR=\"$HOME/Pictures/wallpapers/\"\nNEW_WALL=$(find \"$WALLPAPER_DIR\" -type f \\( -name \"*.jpg\" -o -name \"*.png\" -o -name \"*.webp\" \\) | shuf -n 1)\n\n# 2. Update the symlink to point to the new wallpaper\nln -sf \"$NEW_WALL\" ~/.config/hypr/current_wallpaper.png\n\n# 3. Tell hyprpaper to unload unused images, preload the symlink target, and apply it\nhyprctl hyprpaper preload \"~/.config/hypr/current_wallpaper.png\"\nhyprctl hyprpaper wallpaper \"eDP-1, ~/.config/hypr/current_wallpaper.png\"\nhyprctl hyprpaper unload unused\n```\n\nMake the script executable:\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/bin/switch-wallpaper.sh\n```\n\n### Tips & Caveats:\n* **Caching/Preloading quirks:** `hyprpaper` caches images in memory based on their path. Because your symlink path (`current_wallpaper.png`) remains constant while the underlying file changes, `hyprpaper` might occasionally hold onto the old image buffer unless you use `hyprctl hyprpaper reload` or clear unused assets properly via `unload`.\n* **Alternative (Native Directories):** Recent versions of `hyprpaper` natively support passing a directory path directly into the configuration block (allowing automated timed rotations without manual scripting)[4]. However, if you prefer manual control, random hot-swapping, or integration with CLI pickers (like `waybar`, `rofi`, or `wofi`), the symlink method remains heavily utilized.\n\nSources:\n[1] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHF1rJLZ9eJldKTeWK6cKZM0-2tL-iIFKhujiQQ4CIn9W1U7HjfhSQbb32I3p5jEWYz1k3EdDxCMvqHnlVAV4dXSBMTXdT4ygb3JdA57ukP3oTWrUfF2Xz1ADIbbOBtMe0jr0f8)\n[2] [hyprland.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFRXwozsvvRwpJQUNVP5xhQuGp__eWoKfr2m8wm_CyKOt5zSn65O759M0ApdrbVAPuCePZ-gx0yHdAcvK5FwecVFREhENMBwqbKISpEnahPKIFInDS4QxtsHq1k_AV0uYjcoZbp-UkglPcYqm5QUTyU)\n[3] [linuxcommandlibrary.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGu0iq0CoEjZz3vNGh15I2dskZI3Dw3w__h4c5zOJuhUEYB7IM4B-5ZV1ECHv7_yuru2c03mKrvb3XvvgebAsDSmBF6VAQHzRkV7Ju6e8qnT-LjzY1VhVL7lw80Y_pZxPZITC4=)\n[4] [hypr.land](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFqWtaRqTbfTi11rpc0yh8DNLhABfu47BsXTOThHBPcCbFLW8e5TOeu8rJ0NKSiAyJmgRts4bv7ZfS0EqClpQBJOn4l5157DQwDXNGk97hHQmsFcyK8U0xK5ArY7mreoM0DQyWYcL6VIu_lDw==)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
{"step_index":92,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T10:19:32Z","input_tokens":2161,"cache_read_tokens":50024,"output_tokens":731,"thinking":"Initial hypothesis: wallpaper might not be refreshing due to caching. The warning suggests `hyprpaper` might cache the image based on the symlink path. This means updates to the underlying file won't be reflected without a reload or cache clearing. Considering if the boot process initially loads the wallpaper path directly or if the `hyprctl hyprpaper reload` command with the real file path can resolve the issue.\n\n\nThe current setup in `pywal.sh` appears to create a symlink, and the direct `hyprctl hyprpaper` call might bypass the caching issue by using the actual file path. This approach ensures `hyprpaper` loads the correct wallpaper immediately, while the symlink maintains consistency for future boots or restarts. This eliminates the cycling issue, making the wallpaper static until the user runs `pywal`. Testing this involves running `pywal` and observing the wallpaper behavior after updates.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"cd ~/.dotfiles && git status","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Check git status of dotfiles"}}]}
